From: ane-stack
Subject: [PATCH 0017] espresso: decode macOS 27.x's nested loadModel code

Patch 0014 read the FIRST 0x token in the description. Correct on macOS 26.x,
where the code is a terminal parenthesised token and the only hex token present.
WRONG on macOS 27.x, whose message shape is:

  ...error:: Program load failed — bad program image (model may be corrupt or
  incompatible) (underlying=0x13)

There the code is nested after underlying= and may be preceded by other
parenthesised text. Measured on a real macOS 27.0 box: the old rule returned
Some(19) and classified a genuine deploy refusal as Unrecognised with
form=None, silently discarding the code.

take_code now uses one locator array with explicit precedence, most specific
first, each returning the digit-run offset:
  1. underlying=0x...   — macOS 27.x's nested code (unambiguous marker)
  2. terminal (0x...)   — macOS 26.x, via rfind so selector-text parens lose
  3. first 0x token     — last resort for an unknown shape

Also corrects a test that encoded the old assumption. only_the_first_hex_token_
is_read asserted the first token wins, predating 27.x, which gets 26.x-style
descriptions wrong once an incidental earlier hex token exists. Renamed to
parenthesised_trailing_code_beats_earlier_hex_token, reason recorded inline per
ENGINEERING.md 3.1 — the original intent (selector text must not win) is
subsumed by the corrected rule, and the 27.x shape test is the one that would
have caught the original bug.

Both message shapes are pinned by tests; indistinguishable without OS-specific
provenance, so neither may be dropped.

Refactored after first landing at the lane owner's direction: the three
branches duplicated the extract-and-parse logic. First attempt left a dead
offset table and an unused binding, caught by re-reading the diff rather than
by the compiler (rustc warns on unused variables, not on a dead `let` that is
then discarded); removed in the same pass. Verified on hardware on BOTH OSes:
macOS 26.7.1 still decodes 0x170004 x2 and 0x130004; macOS 27.0 now reads 0x13
for a real h16g/h17 model pair and Apple's H16/H17 bundles.

27.x refusals still land on Unrecognised, deliberately: the new cause text is
not in the measured table, and inventing a cause for an unmeasured code is
worse than admitting ignorance.

Evidence: docs/evidence/PROBE-2-macos27-result.md
